</div>"is a/the brain" cannot be paraphrased as "does brain" with the same<=
br>
semantics in English... that doesn't say enough about whether "besna"<br>
itself could have such an interpretative aspect at all.<br>
<br>
I said "function-wise". A lid has a function as well as it's an<br>
object; "ko'a gacri" can be understood as "koha is a lid (for<br>
something)" or "koha covers (something)". A brain has a function as<br>
well as it's an object; I can understand "ko'a besna" as "koha is a<br>
brain (of something)" or "koha [brain-VERB] (for something)".<br>
Something besna something. The question is:<br>
 &nbsp;-- Would "a rhino", "you", etc. represent only the latter<br>
"something", or only the former "something", or both?<br>
To put it differently:<br>
 &nbsp;-- Would the word "you" fail to represent you if you became nothing<=
br>
but a (living) besna1?<br>
Imagine a world with super-advanced cybernetics that allows easy<br>
transfer of brains between shells. The official definition assumes<br>
that x2, the shell, is essential to the meaning of "brain", and I find<br>
that to potentially be not the case.<br>
<div><br>
<br>
